# E14731 collaborator draft; formal ID subject to editor review.
id: E14731
trigger_tool: sh|mksh|bash|zsh|dash
phase: runtime
error_signature: cd-on-exit stdout is ''|exit trap output missing from stdout|\$\(shfm\) is empty
severity: 2
category: shell, portability, ohos, runtime, posix
match_keywords: EXIT trap, trap EXIT, exit inside function, redirected function, main >/dev/tty, cd-on-exit, cd on exit, shfm, mksh, dash, bash, zsh, stdout empty, fd 3
title: EXIT trap output of a redirected shell function reaches the tty in mksh/bash/zsh (only dash restores stdout first), so cd-on-exit idioms like cd "$(shfm)" get an empty string
root_cause: In mksh (the OHOS /bin/sh), bash (also --posix) and zsh the redirect of a function call such as `main "$@" >/dev/tty` is still active when the EXIT trap runs after `exit` inside that function, so `printf '%s\n' "$PWD" >&1` in the trap lands on /dev/tty; only dash pops the redirect before the trap and prints to the caller's stdout. shfm 0.4.2 prints its cd-on-exit directory exactly this way, so `$(shfm)` is empty under mksh/bash/zsh while the same script passes on a Debian host whose /bin/sh is dash.
affected_scope: POSIX-sh programs that redirect their whole body (`main "$@" >/dev/tty`, `{ ...; } >file`) and emit a machine-readable final result (cd-on-exit directory, exit summary) from an EXIT or INT trap to the caller's stdout, e.g. shfm 0.4.2 and similar TUI file managers; traps that write to stderr or to a file are not affected.
verification_cmd: for sh in dash bash mksh zsh; do printf '%s -> [' $sh; $sh -c 'f() { trap "echo TRAP" EXIT; exit 0; }; f >/dev/null'; echo ']'; done   # only dash prints TRAP; with `exec 3>&1` before the call and `echo TRAP >&3` all four print it
rec_priority: 1
rec_approach: save-stdout-fd3-before-redirect
rec_description: Save the caller's stdout once at top level (`exec 3>&1`) before the redirected call, make the trap write to `>&3`, and start child processes with `3>&-` so they cannot keep the capture pipe open. Prove it with a pty-driven consumer test that runs the packaged script under bash (and mksh when available) and asserts the stdout of the cd-on-exit capture.
rec_command: exec 3>&1; main "$@" >/dev/tty   # in the trap: printf '%s\n' "$PWD" >&3 ; children: cmd 3>&-
rec_auto_fix: false
rec_impl: manual-source-fix
fallback_priority: 2
fallback_approach: print-result-after-main-returns
fallback_description: Turn the `exit` calls into `return` and print the result at top level after `main "$@" >/dev/tty` has returned, instead of from a trap.
fallback_command: main "$@" >/dev/tty; printf '%s\n' "$PWD"
fallback_auto_fix: false
fallback_impl: manual-source-fix
forbid_1_pattern: verify cd-on-exit only with dash|test the packaged script only through /bin/sh on a Debian host
forbid_1_reason: dash is the one shell that restores stdout before the EXIT trap, so it cannot show the defect, whereas OHOS uses mksh and the hnp bash.
forbid_1_consequence: The package passes CI on a dash host but `cd "$(shfm)"` stays empty on the device.
principles: P005
similar_to: []
conflicts_with: []
prerequisite_of: []
